Well, figuring out how much one can carry is just a matter of picking up
one item at a time until one becomes "strained" and then dropping the
last item.

As for deciding what to drop to become un-burdened, you could do what I
do.  There are two classes of items in my mind:  stuff you need to use
RIGHT NOW and stuff that you can take the time to haul out of a bag. 
Stuff in the first category includes your primary weapon as well as
(maybe) a missile weapon, your armor, some assorted wands, scrolls and
potions to get you out of trouble (e.g. attacking wands like death,
cold, fire, lightening and magic missile, a wand or two of teleportation
and digging to vacate the premises in a hurry, scrolls of taming and
scare monster, cursed potions of gain level, potions of healing and
extra healing...the list goes on), your rings, your amulet, your unicorn
horn, your key (or credit card or lock pick) and your pick axe.  Now,
obvious you usually don't NEED to use your pick or key RIGHT NOW, but
you use them often enough that you don't want to haul them out of the
bag all the time.

Everything else I always keep in my bag of holding, or if I don't have
one, in a normal sack.  This includes all my other tools (stethescopes,
tinning kits, magic markers, etc), all those spare scrolls, potions and
wands, my gems, my gold and any backup weapons and armor I might have. 
That way, if I want to get unburdened in a hurry, I can just dump my bag
and voila! (french, meaning "it's time to kick butt")  This has the
added advantage that the stupid monsters can't go rooting through your
sack to try and find something they can use, but if you just dumped
stuff on the ground willy-nilly, they could haul out that wand of fire
you forgot about and go to town.  The final advantage is that it cleans
up my inventory screen.  If I'm getting stomped on my a bunch of
monsters, and it's time to go looking through the ol' inventory for a
miracle, I don't want to have to wade through everything to try and find
that one special wand I need.

